    "q-156194": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156194",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284204/assets/1a3b8289936cd2597ac1252e2b28987f245a7c4e88dcf3677e1ba3626460688b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284204/assets/b5becb438f3bc8ab5445e116ddb64bf168013301838231bdc4d32d34283da5f1.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+5=\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
