    "q-103770": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103770",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284204/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284204/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
