    "q-345060": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345060",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The volume, in gallons, of water in a tank $t$ minutes after filling begins increases at a rate of $2{t}^{2}+8.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the tank contained $15$ gallons of water, how much water will the tank contain after $3$ minutes?\n\nAfter $3$ minutes, the tank contains\n{{field-1}} gallons of water.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $a(t)=2{t}^{2}+8$ be the rate at which the amount of water in the tank is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of water in the tank $T$ minutes after filling begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}2{t}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}+8t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+(\\frac{2}{3}{T}^{3}+8T)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =15+\\frac{2}{3}{T}^{3}+8T \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{T}^{3}+8T+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of water in the tank $T=3$ minutes after filling begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(3)=15+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}+8\\cdot 3=57.\n$$\n\nThus, after $3$ minutes, the tank contains $57$ gallons of water.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
